Unlike wealth, which always begs the question 'what for?', happiness is a natural stopping point — it explains itself by being what it is.
Mill's distinction between higher and lower pleasures — intellectual versus sensory — acknowledges that the content of happiness matters, but still places happiness as the frame within which these distinctions operate.
Even critics of hedonistic happiness (Nozick's Experience Machine argument) are arguing for a richer conception of happiness, not against happiness's ultimate importance — the debate is about what happiness really is.
